course: dictionary
lesson: 11521
title: Allege: a claim, not yet proof
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To allege something is to say it is true, especially that someone did something wrong, but without proving it yet. «The police allege that the man broke the shop window.» The police are saying he did it, but they have not shown proof yet.
explain: Look for the clue words. «It is alleged that» and «the alleged thief» both show that a claim has not been proved. Someone who is alleged to have done something may still be innocent until the proof is found.
explain: Ask yourself: has someone shown proof, or is this only a claim? If it is only a claim, allege is the right word. If it has been proved, you would not need to allege it.
fact: Allege is a verb: allege, alleges, alleged, alleging.
fact: «Allege» means to say something is true, often that someone did something wrong, without proof yet.
fact: «Alleged» shows a claim that has not been proved, as in «the alleged thief».
fact: An accusation is a claim that someone did wrong; an accusation is not proof.
fact: A good check is to swap in the word «claims» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The police allege that the driver ran the red light.» What does allege mean here? | They have proved it beyond doubt | They are sure it is a lie | *They say it is true, but have not proved it yet | They saw it happen on a film
exercise: «The alleged thief was let go because there was no proof.» Why is the word alleged used? | It means the thief was found guilty | The thief is a famous person | *The claim has not been proved | The thief has confessed
exercise: «Yesterday, the newspaper ___ that the mayor took money.» Which word fits the gap? | *alleged | alleging | allege | alleges
test: Which sentence shows that the claim has not been proved? | The robber was caught on camera and admitted it. | *«The alleged robber is waiting for the court to decide.» | The thief was sent to prison for the theft. | The evidence proved the robber was guilty.
test: Dad says, «Your brother alleges you ate the last cake.» What does he mean? | Your brother ate the cake himself | *Your brother says you ate it, but it is not proved | Your brother has proved that you ate it | You were given the cake as a prize
exam: In «The school alleged that Ben had cheated on the test», what is the best swap for alleged? | *Claimed, but not yet proved | Proved | Forgiven | Hidden
